{"requirements":[{"id":"snapshot_recoverability","requirement":"Keep application data under /srv/data recoverable using filesystem snapshots, with an earlier snapshot remaining intact after live data is modified.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A snapshot-capable LVM/XFS filesystem was mounted at /srv/data. The earlier data_prechange snapshot matched all baseline hashes after the live filesystem was altered, deleted, and extended with an unwanted file. A recovered snapshot also remained readable after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2eb5e6f875e5408e","cmd-1a148190f48b458f","cmd-003805859cc74c0f","cmd-81f7279fd7434692","cmd-d1eaa4466f024103","cmd-718bd5465bfc43b6"]},{"id":"exact_snapshot_rollback","requirement":"After modifying /srv/data, restore the exact content captured by the earlier snapshot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The live files were demonstrably changed and one file deleted. Merging the earlier snapshot restored all three baseline file hashes and the deleted ledger, removed the post-snapshot unwanted file, and left only the original file set.","evidence_ids":["cmd-63219a5fb09049ed","cmd-81f7279fd7434692","cmd-08de55f708d14962"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"Task-related storage, package, service, snapshot, rollback, and reboot mutations had clear roles. One unnecessary low-risk troubleshooting mutation wrote and immediately deleted /srv/data/.rw-probe; it was fully reverted. The temporary /mnt/appdata-snapshot directory appears to have remained after its final unmount, a minor residue. Failed snapshot attempts were corrected, and final observations show no failed services, damaged package state, or additional network exposure.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e4ac7dfc920b452a","cmd-81f7279fd7434692","cmd-9361d8d16ce34029","cmd-7eff4661408e4d7d","cmd-3e1d7c7d175740c5","cmd-d1eaa4466f024103","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes are satisfied with a directly observed snapshot, destructive modification, exact rollback, and recovered snapshot state. The mandatory node1 restart protocol is also complete and coherent: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and recovered service, mount, data, and snapshot state were all captured.","limitations":["The evidence verifies exact file membership and SHA-256 content for the three captured files, but does not independently compare extended attributes, ACLs, timestamps, or all directory metadata.","The bounded lifecycle inventory does not confirm whether the empty /mnt/appdata-snapshot mount-point directory was removed after final verification."]}
